      "commit": {
        "parents": [
          {
            "commit": "c712bbd46e4794cefa73282ec171a2a7ec42634a",
            "subject": "LU-20791 efalnd: Make connection last use time atomic"
          }
        ],
        "author": {
          "name": "Yonatan Nachum",
          "email": "ynachum@amazon.com",
          "date": "2026-09-22 07:51:14.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Yonatan Nachum",
          "email": "ynachum@amazon.com",
          "date": "2026-10-05 14:55:23.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-20791 efalnd: Don't update last use time on init conn inspection",
        "message": "LU-20791 efalnd: Don't update last use time on init conn inspection\n\nLooking up a connection updates its last use time, which is what keeps\nthe idle timeout from expiring. That is correct when the caller is about\nto send or receive on the connection, but the probe handler also looks\nup the initiator connection purely to check whether it has gone stale\nand needs to be deactivated. When the epoch matches nothing else happens\nto that connection, yet its idle timeout has just been extended.\n\nThe effect is that a peer which keeps probing keeps our initiator\nconnection alive even though we never use it, holding on to the device\nresources it owns. A connection's idle timeout should reflect our own\ntraffic, not the peer's.\n\nPass whether the lookup should update the last use time, and have the\nprobe handler ask for a lookup that leaves it alone. Every other caller\nis about to use the connection and keeps the current behaviour.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nSigned-off-by: Yonatan Nachum <ynachum@amazon.com>\nChange-Id: I76b37c7d195cad036e9e2ede1be2765847a1b4f8\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
